Brute Force
-----------
Idea:
- Compare characters column by column using the first string as a reference.
- Stop when a mismatch is found or one string ends.

Algorithm:
1. Take the first string as the reference.
2. Traverse each character of the first string.
3. For every other string:
   - If the current index is out of bounds or characters differ, return the prefix built so far.
4. If all characters match, append the character to the answer.
5. Return the final prefix.

Time Complexity: O(n × m)
Space Complexity: O(1)

Where:
- n = number of strings
- m = length of the shortest string

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    string longestCommonPrefix(vector<string>& strs) {
        string ans = "";

        for (int i = 0; i < strs[0].size(); i++) {
            char ch = strs[0][i];

            for (int j = 1; j < strs.size(); j++) {
                if (i >= strs[j].size() || strs[j][i] != ch)
                    return ans;
            }

            ans += ch;
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
```

Better Solution
---------------
Idea:
- Sort the strings lexicographically.
- After sorting, only the first and last strings need to be compared.
- Their common prefix is the common prefix of the entire array.

Algorithm:
1. Sort the array of strings.
2. Compare the first and last strings character by character.
3. Append matching characters to the answer.
4. Stop at the first mismatch.
5. Return the answer.

Time Complexity: O(n log n × m)
Space Complexity: O(1)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    string longestCommonPrefix(vector<string>& strs) {
        sort(strs.begin(), strs.end());

        string first = strs.front();
        string last = strs.back();

        string ans = "";

        for (int i = 0; i < min(first.size(), last.size()); i++) {
            if (first[i] != last[i])
                break;

            ans += first[i];
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
```

Optimal Solution
----------------
Idea:
- Start with the first string as the current prefix.
- Compare it with every other string.
- Keep shrinking the prefix until it matches the beginning of the current string.

Algorithm:
1. Initialize prefix = strs[0].
2. For each remaining string:
   - While the string does not start with prefix:
     - Remove the last character from prefix.
3. If prefix becomes empty, return "".
4. Return prefix.

Time Complexity: O(n × m)
Space Complexity: O(1)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    string longestCommonPrefix(vector<string>& strs) {
        string prefix = strs[0];

        for (int i = 1; i < strs.size(); i++) {
            while (strs[i].find(prefix) != 0) {
                prefix.pop_back();

                if (prefix.empty())
                    return "";
            }
        }

        return prefix;
    }
};
```

Interview Notes
---------------
- The vertical scanning approach compares one character position across all strings.
- Sorting works because the maximum difference appears between the lexicographically smallest and largest strings.
- The prefix-shrinking approach avoids sorting and keeps reducing the candidate prefix until it matches every string.
- Example:
  - ["flower", "flow", "flight"]
  - Start with "flower"
  - Compare with "flow" → "flow"
  - Compare with "flight" → "fl"
- Edge Cases:
  - Only one string.
  - Empty string in the array.
  - No common prefix.
  - All strings are identical.
- Follow-up:
  - Can this be solved using a Trie?
    - Yes. Insert all strings into a Trie and traverse until reaching a branching node or the end of a word.
- The prefix-shrinking approach is the standard interview solution because it avoids sorting while maintaining O(n × m) time and O(1) extra space.
